      "math_academy_id": "q-155548",
      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216627/assets/867e0d53894e1cacac86120e5c79b32071e2b0a4863b445e6d3492392a59461d.png)\n\nConsider the figure shown above. Which of the following triplets of points are collinear?\n\nI. $M,Q,J$\n\nII. $P,O,L$\n\nIII. $L,O,M$",
      "worked_solution": "- $M,Q,J$ are **not** collinear since we can't draw a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216627/assets/7f707d8fbeb9853ccb9441bbdab3b0674c5f51da7f73da73d73ee6592dca04ea.png)\n\n- $P,O,L$ are **not** collinear since we can't draw a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216627/assets/028ae56c8a85699d4d6719df2f80a989f1271e392546d005093319af2365675d.png)\n\n \n\n- $L,O,M$ are collinear since there is a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216627/assets/bf0579ecbc33ebe2d97dc1b305de0a89b0c22dadd6a11d0e88ac4a36acbd447f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
